<p>See the <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRC guide</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline conveyor guide</a>, and <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for detail on each option.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Spiral Conveyors Work</h2>
<p>A spiral conveyor carries a conveying surface, typically a slat chain, plastic modular belt, or wire mesh belt, along a helical track around a central column or drum. The track rises (or descends) at a constant pitch. Products loaded at the bottom travel up through the helix and discharge at the top, or the reverse for down-spirals.</p>
<h3>Drive Types</h3>
<p><strong>Drum-driven (friction) spirals:</strong> a rotating central drum or cage drives the belt's inner edge by friction. This design is common in spiral freezers, coolers, and proofers, where the belt carries product through many tiers inside an enclosure.</p>
<p><strong>Positive-drive spirals:</strong> a drive unit engages a slat chain or modular belt directly and pulls it through the helix. This design is common in spirals that move cases, totes, and packaged goods between levels. Ask which drive a quote uses, because it affects belt tension, wear parts, and maintenance access.</p>
<h3>Spiral Geometry</h3>
<p>Key dimensions are outside diameter, belt width, number of tiers (wraps), rise per tier, and total elevation change. Pitch must be gentle enough that products do not slide or tip, and the inside radius must suit product length so long items do not overhang or jam. Tighter spirals save floor space but limit belt width and product size. Suppliers publish these limits for their standard models.</p>
<h2 id="modular-heading">Modular vs. Custom Spirals</h2>
<p>A modular spiral conveyor is assembled from standard, factory-built components: tier sections, support columns, a drive unit, and infeed and discharge ends. Height changes by adding or removing tiers, and the unit can be moved or reconfigured when the line changes. The tradeoff is that sizes are limited to the supplier's standard widths, diameters, and pitches.</p>
<p>Custom-engineered spirals are designed around one application, such as a spiral freezer sized to its enclosure or a spiral for unusually heavy or long products. They fit cases that standard modules cannot, at the cost of more engineering time and less flexibility later.</p>
<p>"Modular spiral" is also used for spirals that run on <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">modular plastic belt</a>. Confirm which meaning a supplier intends.</p>
<h2 id="supports-heading">Spiral Conveyor Supports</h2>
<p>Most spiral conveyors are self-supporting: a central column or drum and a ring of outer posts carry the helical track, and the base anchors to the floor. No building steel is needed in most installations, but the support design still drives floor loading, footprint, and access. Check these points before layout is final:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Floor slab:</strong> confirm slab capacity for the load concentrated at the spiral base and the supplier's anchor pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Height and bracing:</strong> tall units, units that pass through a mezzanine or floor opening, and units in seismic zones may need lateral bracing to building structure. Local building code governs.</li>
<li><strong>Infeed and discharge ends:</strong> define who supplies the support stands and how the top discharge ties into the mezzanine or overhead conveyor.</li>
<li><strong>Access and guarding:</strong> outer post spacing and guard panels determine where crews can reach the belt. Guard infeed, discharge, and drive pinch points per <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 1910.212</a> and <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Frame material:</strong> painted or galvanized steel for dry areas. Stainless steel with open, cleanable framing for washdown.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="updown-heading">Spiral Up vs. Spiral Down</h2>
<h3>Spiral Up Conveyors</h3>
<p>Spiral up conveyors lift cases from a floor-level packaging line to an overhead conveyor, a mezzanine palletizing area, or an elevated shipping conveyor. They need more drive power than spiral down units because they work against gravity plus friction.</p>
<h3>Spiral Down Conveyors</h3>
<p>Spiral down conveyors lower products from an overhead conveyor or mezzanine to floor-level shipping. In <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a>, the long spiral path gives hot products extended dwell time in cooled or ambient air.</p>
<h3>Dual Spirals</h3>
<p>Dual spiral conveyors combine an up-spiral and a down-spiral, nested or side by side, to provide both elevation change and a return path in one footprint. They suit processes where products go up through an overhead zone (cooling, drying, curing) and return to the original line.</p>
<h2 id="mode-heading">Continuous vs. Indexed Operation</h2>
<h3>Continuous Flow</h3>
<p>Continuous-flow spirals run at constant speed with products entering and exiting simultaneously. Throughput equals belt speed divided by product pitch. For example, a belt at 100 FPM carrying products on 18-inch pitch delivers about 67 products per minute. Mass-flow spirals carry unspaced product, such as bottles or cans, back to back rather than on a set pitch.</p>
<h3>Indexed Operation</h3>
<p>Indexed spirals stop and start to fill the spiral before advancing. This maximizes the number of products in the spiral at any time and the holding time per product. It suits cooling and freezing applications where dwell time matters more than throughput rate.</p>
<h3>Variable Speed</h3>
<p>Variable-speed drives adjust spiral speed to upstream production rate and downstream demand. The spiral runs faster during peak production and slower when longer dwell time (cooling, drying) is needed. This is common in bakery spiral coolers where rate changes between products.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Spiral Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line elevation changes:</strong> spirals lift finished cases, cartons, and totes from floor-level packaging to mezzanine palletizing or overhead <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>, using far less floor space than a long <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline conveyor</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Food cooling and freezing:</strong> spirals carry bakery products, prepared meals, and meat through cooling or freezing chambers. The spiral path provides long dwell time inside a compact enclosure. Stainless steel frames and food-grade belts are standard.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center level changes:</strong> spirals move totes and cartons between floors in multi-level distribution and fulfillment centers, where batch-lifting VRCs would limit throughput.</p>
<p><strong>Vertical buffering:</strong> a spiral holds a long run of conveyor in a small footprint, so slowing or stopping it accumulates product in the helix. Dynamic buffer spirals vary their storage length with line conditions. See <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">buffer conveyors</a> for FIFO and LIFO buffer designs and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a> for horizontal options.</p>
<p><strong>Proofing and drying:</strong> spirals carry dough through temperature- and humidity-controlled proofers and carry coated or washed products through drying zones. The helix maximizes dwell time per unit of chamber volume.</p>
<p><strong>Clean room and pharmaceutical:</strong> enclosed stainless spirals move product between clean room levels or between production and packaging areas at different elevations.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Specify a Spiral Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Elevation change and floor space set diameter and tier count.</strong> Total rise divided by rise per tier gives the number of tiers. A larger diameter allows more rise per tier and wider belts but uses more floor space.</p>
<p><strong>Product size and weight set belt width and type.</strong> The product must fit the belt with side clearance, and load per foot must stay within the belt and drive rating. Heavy products may call for a heavier-duty positive-drive spiral or a <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRC</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Throughput sets belt speed and product pitch.</strong> Products per minute equals belt speed divided by pitch (product length plus gap). <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> at infeed and discharge must match spiral speed for smooth handoff.</p>
<p><strong>Environment sets materials and belt type.</strong> Dry packaging: steel frame, slat or plastic modular belt. Washdown food: stainless frame, open belt for drainage. Freezer: stainless frame with a belt rated for the operating temperature. High heat: heat-rated belt and lubrication.</p>
<p><strong>Supports and access need early decisions.</strong> Confirm slab capacity, bracing, and who supplies infeed and discharge supports. Specify removable guards or walk-in access for belt cleaning and replacement, since belt replacement on a tall spiral is a major maintenance event.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a spiral conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A vertical conveyor that moves products between levels along a helical belt path around a central column. Products enter and exit at the same time, so flow stays continuous. See the <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor type guide</a> for all vertical transport options.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a modular spiral conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A spiral assembled from standard tier sections, columns, a drive unit, and infeed and discharge ends. Height changes by adding or removing tiers, and the unit can be relocated. Sizes are limited to the supplier's standard range. The term also describes spirals that run on modular plastic belt, so confirm the meaning.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How are spiral conveyors supported?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Most are self-supporting: a central column or drum and outer posts carry the track, and the base anchors to the floor. Tall units, units passing through a mezzanine, and units in seismic zones may need bracing to building structure. Confirm slab capacity, anchoring, and infeed and discharge supports.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Spiral conveyor vs VRC, which should I choose?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Spiral for continuous, high-throughput flow of cases, cartons, and totes. <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRC</a> for pallets and heavy loads, lower throughput, and a smaller footprint. Spirals cost more but eliminate batch-cycling delays.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">How much does a spiral conveyor cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Spirals are a significant capital investment compared with incline conveyors or VRCs. Cost depends on elevation, belt width, tier count, drive type, environment (washdown, freezer), and construction material. They justify cost through continuous throughput and small footprint. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for detailed factors.</p>

</div>
</div>
